    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\oint_C xy~dx + x^2~dy</m> where <m>C</m> is the curve shown and
  oriented in the counterclockwise direction.
            </p>
             <image width="35%">
            <shortdescription>A curve C oriented in the counterclockwise direction.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-hw3-1">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,180)" margins="5">
                  <definition>g(x)=1/sqrt(x)</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,5,1.5)">
                        <grid-axes />
                       <line endpoints="((1,1),(1,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="4"/>
                       <line endpoints="((1,0),(4,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="4" arrows="1"/>
                       <line endpoints="((4,0),(4,0.5))" stroke="blue" thickness="4"/>
                       <graph function="g" thickness="4" domain="(1,4)"/>
                       <label anchor="(2.5,1)"><m>y = \frac1{\sqrt{x}}</m></label>

                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
           </image>

        </statement>

        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use Green's theorem to evaluate <m>\ds \int_C e^x\cos y~dx - e^x\sin
  y~dy</m> where <m>C</m> is the curve composed of two straight line segments from
  <m>A(\ln 2, 0)</m> to <m>B(0,1)</m> and from <m>B(0,1)</m> to <m>C(-\ln 2,0)</m>.
            </p>

        
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>C</m> is a simple closed curve in the plane that
  bounds the region <m>D</m>.  

            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the integral
  <md>
  \frac12\oint_C -y~dx+ x~dy = \mbox{Area}(D).
  </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>C</m> is the line segment from <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> to
  <m>(x_2,y_2)</m>.  Show that
<md>
  \int_C -y~dx + x~dy = x_1y_2 - x_2y_1 = 
  \left|\begin{array}{cc}
      x_1 \amp x_2 \\
      y_1 \amp y_2
      \end{array}\right|.
</md>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>P</m> is a polygon in the plane whose vertices, traversed
  in counterclockwise order, are <m>(x_1,y_1)</m>, <m>(x_2,y_2)</m>, ...
  <m>(x_n,y_n)</m>.  Show (hint use part b) that the area of <m>P</m> is
  <md>
  \frac12\left[(x_1y_2-x_2y_1) + (x_2y_3-x_3y_2) + \ldots +
  (x_{n-1}y_n-x_ny_{n-1}) + (x_ny_1-x_1y_n)\right].
  </md>

                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch the pentagon whose vertices are <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(2,1)</m>,
  <m>(1,3)</m>, <m>(0,2)</m> and <m>(-1,0)</m> and find its area. (Hint use part c.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An ellipse whose semi-axes have length <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> may be
  parametrized by <m>x=a\cos t</m> and <m>y=b\sin t</m> where <m>0\le t\le 2\pi</m>.  Show that this ellipse
  has area <m>\pi ab</m>. (Hint use part a.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's explore a different identity.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show <em>Green's first identity</em>, assuming 
                    the usual notation: <m>C</m> is a simple closed curve and <m>D</m>
                    is the region it bounds:
                    <md>
                        \oint_C f(\nabla g)\cdot \mbf n~ds = \iint_D f\nabla^2 g ~dA + \iint_D \nabla f \cdot \nabla g ~dA.
                    </md>
                    You will use this identity in parts (b) and (c).  Hint: Recall from homework 2 
                    you proved an identity that may be very helpful here.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <em>harmonic function</em> <m>g</m> is one for which <m>\nabla^2 g = 0</m>.  Using 
                    part (a) and choosing <m>f</m> very carefully, show that, if <m>g</m> is harmonic then
                    <md>
                        \oint_C \nabla g \cdot \mbf n~ ds = 0.
                    </md>
                    We will see later that the steady-state temperature distribution 
  in a region is a harmonic function.  We'll also see that
  the fact above shows the intuitively obvious fact that, in the
  steady-state, the net flow of heat into the region must be zero.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>f</m> is harmonic and <m>f(x,y) = 0</m> on the
  curve <m>C</m>.  Show that
  <md>
  \iint_D|\nabla f|^2~dA = 0.
  </md>
  and explain why this means that <m>f(x,y) = 0</m> at every point inside the
  region <m>D</m>.
</p><p>

  Again, this makes intuitive sense: if the steady-state temperature
  is constantly zero on the boundary of a region, we should expect
  that the temperature is zero at every point inside the region.

                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the vector field <m>\mbf F = (y, x-3)</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify that <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    After choosing the basepoint <m>*=(0,0)</m>,
  create a potential function <m>f(x,y)</m> by integrating from the
  basepoint to a general point <m>(x,y)</m>.
  (You can probably guess what <m>f</m> is, but I would like for you
  to find it by integrating.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     If one of your classmates were to choose a different
  basepoint, how would your two functions differ?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose we have an infinitely long electrically
  charged cylindrical pipe whose charge density is <m>\rho</m> Coulombs
  per cubic meter.  That is, supposed we have a 
  <em>solid</em> cylinder with charge equally distributed 
  <em>throughout the whole cylinder</em>.  Assume <m>\rho</m> 
  is positive (electric field points away from positive charge and 
  towards negative charge), and assume the radius of the cylinder is 
  <m>R</m> (and just to emphasize, the cylinder is not hollow). 
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the electric field <m>\vec{E}</m> outside the
  cylindrical solid.  (What would be a useful choice for the Gaussian surface?)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the electric field <m>\vec E</m> inside the cylindrical solid. 
                     (What would be a useful choice for the Gaussian surface?)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a continuous potential function <m>f</m> so that
  <m>\vec{E} = \nabla f</m>.
                </p><p>
  Note:  You will want to create the potential by integrating so that
  the potential you find is continuous.  What may be a good basepoint?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>